# id: SEAM-ignore-short-count
# blinds: the loop resuming from the count write(2) actually returned
# covering-lane: cpp-test
# expect: killed
# control-lane: cpp-build
# expect-control: alive
#
# The loop stops resuming and assumes every write was complete. On a local
# filesystem write(2) essentially always returns the full count, so this is
# correct on every machine anyone runs it on -- until a pipe, a network
# filesystem or a signal makes it not, and the bytes it silently drops are WAL
# bytes that were reported durable.
#
# NOT A CATALOGUE MUTANT. DESIGN-B1 section 14.2 records "Mutants: none" for
# B1.2b, and that is a statement about the BM catalogue: PosixEnv is outside the
# fault matrix, so it gets no BM number. But the standing rule is that no gate
# counts until its failure has been induced and observed, and B1.2b's gates are
# the three seam tests. This patch is that induction, made repeatable rather
# than performed once by hand -- there is no CI here, so an induction nobody can
# re-run is an induction that decays into a sentence in a commit message.
--- a/engine-cpp/src/env/posix/posix_raw.cc	2026-08-24 17:16:49
+++ b/engine-cpp/src/env/posix/posix_raw.cc	2026-08-24 17:16:49
@@ -29,7 +29,7 @@
       continue;
     }
     zeros = 0;
-    done += static_cast<std::size_t>(w);
+    done = n;  // SEAM: assume the whole write landed
   }
   return Status::Ok();
 }
